# EXPECT: a-bXc 1= amp=X|a-b-c amp=X|12x45 amp=3|hello amp=é|kEEp keep amp=e|aBc 1=U amp=b|aBCabc 1= amp=c|aXbXc amp=q1
# A literal s/// (no regex metacharacters, no flags but /g) takes a fast path
# in compiled code (strada_subst_sv_cnt) that never recorded the match, so
# $& (captures()->[0]) kept the previous match's text. It now records the
# first match like the regex path: $& is set and $1..$N are undef (Perl;
# until 2026-11-26 they kept the previous match's groups). A stale group array from an older match is not resurrected.
func amp() str { my $a = captures()->[0]; return defined($a) ? $a : "U"; }
func main() int {
    my @r = ();
    my $s = "aXbXc"; "q1" =~ /(q)(1)/; $s =~ s/X/-/;
    push(@r, $s . " 1=" . $1 . " amp=" . amp());
    $s = "aXbXc"; $s =~ s/X/-/g; push(@r, $s . " amp=" . amp());
    my $t = 12345; $t =~ s/3/x/; push(@r, $t . " amp=" . amp());
    my $u = "héllo"; $u =~ s/é/e/; push(@r, $u . " amp=" . amp());
    my $k = "keep"; my $c = $k; $c =~ s/e/E/g; push(@r, $c . " " . $k . " amp=" . amp());
    my $x = "abc"; "ab" =~ /(a)(b)/; "zz" =~ /z/; $x =~ s/b/B/;
    push(@r, $x . " 1=" . ($1 // "U") . " amp=" . amp());
    $x = "abcabc"; "ab" =~ /(a)(b)/; $x =~ s/b/B/; $x =~ s/c/C/;
    push(@r, $x . " 1=" . $1 . " amp=" . amp());
    $x = "aXbXc"; "q1" =~ /(q)(1)/; $x =~ s/Q/-/; push(@r, $x . " amp=" . amp());
    say(join("|", @r));
    return 0;
}
